<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polyushko-pole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Полюшко-поле</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Polyushko-pole&quot;, known in English as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Plains&quot;, &quot;Meadowlands&quot;, &quot;Cavalry of the Steppes&quot; or &quot;O Fields, My Fields&quot;, is a Soviet song with music by Lev Knipper and words by Viktor Gusev, written in 1933. In Russian pole means a plain or field, and polyushko is its fond, diminutive form.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lev Knipper composed the music and Viktor Gusev wrote the words in 1933. The song became part of Knipper's symphony with chorus A Poem about a Komsomol Soldier, composed in 1934, also with lyrics by Gusev. The original lyrics are sung from the point of view of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recruit who proudly leaves his home to keep watch against the enemies of his homeland.</p><p>Many artists covered the song in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, among them a well-known rock version recorded by Poyushchiye Gitary around 1967. The Alexandrov Ensemble, best known as the Red Army Choir, has performed and recorded it regularly. At the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> 1945 Youth Congress a choir of 6,000 members sang the full version.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> recorded it in 1942 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Plains, sung in English and Russian, and it appeared on his album Songs of Free Men in 1943. The Swedish jazz pianist Jan Johansson recorded it in 1967 as Stepp, min stepp, and Jefferson Airplane included an instrumental Meadowlands on the album Volunteers in 1969. Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the tune is also known as The Cossack Patrol, notably in a version by Ivan Rebroff. The Rahbani brothers reused the melody, crediting Knipper, in their 1974 play Loulou for the song Kanou Ya Habibi, sung by Fairuz.</p><p>The Chabad-Lubavitch Hasidim made a nigun from the tune, sung to the words Der Rebbe Zal Zayn Gezunt, and the German power metal band Powerwolf used it for Werewolves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> on their 2009 album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> of the Beast. Blackmore's Night adapted the melody for Gone with the Wind on their 1999 album Under a Violet Moon.</p><p>The song has been heard in films and on television, among them the opening credits of The Russians Are Coming the Russians Are Coming of 1966, REDS of 1981, Cast Away and the series Kleo. Michael Palin performed it with the choir of the Russian Pacific Fleet in the series Full Circle with Michael Palin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polyushko-pole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
